  "pageshortdescription": {
    "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
    "shortDescTitle": "<p>You treat a wide range of patients—you should have a wide range of solutions<br />\r\n</p>\r\n",
    "shortDescTop": "<p>Our Modular Dual Mobility with X3 precisely engineered polyethylene is the first modular Mobile Bearing Hip System. MDM is designed to help prevent dislocation and assist your operative goals of stability, longevity and advanced fixation for fractures, primary or revision total hip arthroplasty cases. MDM X3 is compatible with all Trident and Trident II acetabular components, offering you a choice of fixation surfaces and screwhole configurations.<br />\r\n</p>\r\n<p>MDM X3 is based on the dual mobility principle with two points of articulation. Dual Mobility bearings marry two well-known concepts in THA: <br />\r\n</p>\r\n",
    "jcr:lastModifiedBy": "hannah.white@stryker.com",
    "hideComponent": "true",
    "jcr:lastModified": "Wed Jan 11 2023 15:32:59 GMT+0000",
    "sling:resourceType": "stryker/components/content/pageshortdescription",
    "textIsRich": [
      "true",
      "true",
      "true"
    ],
    "shortDescBottom": "<p>MDM X3 offers stability with its large head and low-friction design. It has a three-dimensional posterior dislocation distance at 26 degrees of pelvic tilt, that is 59% greater in jump height with MDM compared to a conventional THA with a 36mm head and 81% greater in jump height compared to competitive hard-on-hard bearing.<sup>3</sup> MDM X3 Advanced Bearing Technology is designed to minimize the risk of wear, which may prolong the life of the implant and offer your patients greater implant longevity.<sup>4,5</sup> Clinical performance of X3 in an RSA Trident study of 21 patients has demonstrated mean linear wear rate of 0.001mm/yr (1 micron) at five years.<sup>6</sup><br />\r\n</p>\r\n",
    "bulletList": {
      "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
      "1": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "bullet": "<p>The Charnley&nbsp;low-friction arthroplasty (LFA) prosthesis has demonstrated clinically and radiologically that smaller diameter heads produce lower torque force in the acetabular shell and consequently less wear.<sup>1,2</sup><br>\r\n</p>\r\n",
        "textIsRich": "true"
      },
      "2": {
        "jcr:primaryType": "nt:unstructured",
        "bullet": "<p>The large head concept from McKee-Farrar recognizes that a large diameter bearing is inherently more stable than a smaller diameter head.<sup>1</sup><br>\r\n</p>\r\n",
        "textIsRich": "true"
      }
    }
  },
